		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: 100% guarantee non-interpenetration physics ?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=163#p163"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Stephane, do you 100% guarantee non-interpenetration physics using your approach with ccd, given your external tolerances ?</div></blockquote>Yes. Provided interval arithmetic is implemented correctly, the complete algorithm guarantees 100% non-interpenetration physics. The reason is that each motion (i.e. free motion, constrained motion, or displacements to attempt to reposition the objects and slightly separate them from each other) is only considered as 'intentional' and is subject to CCD. At worst, the CCD will prevent the objects to move completely, but will never allow them to penetrate.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6">Stephane Redon</a> — Thu Jul 28, 2005 11:02 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
